WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.876
Welcome to Cauldron Lane!

2
00:00:03.876 --> 00:00:09.844
In this video you will learn how to play by watching a real game against the computer, step by step.

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.929
You run a potion shop on a busy lane: buy herbs, hunt magical creatures for rare parts,

4
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:21.283
brew potions, and sell them to the townsfolk, or to the shop next door.

5
00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:26.447
Every action costs days, and when the calendar reaches the Moon Fair on day twenty-eight,

6
00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:28.255
the richest shop wins.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.633
This is the home page, with buttons for the game lobby and the music.

8
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:40.840
First pick your opponents: computer shops, or up to four players sharing this device.

9
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.797
Online play comes at launch.

10
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.979
You can face one to three rival shops. One is the easiest to follow.

11
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.380
Name your shop and pick the difficulty.

12
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:58.049
Hard plans every day carefully, and opens with three extra coins. We will take it on.

13
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.549
We will open first. Below are the rulebook and your game history. Let’s open the shops!

14
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.331
At the top: day zero of twenty-eight, the next market day, and the Moon Fair, when the game ends.

15
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.975
These are the two shops: coins, the day each has reached, customers waiting,

16
00:01:16.975 --> 00:01:21.413
potions on the shelf, and customers served. We start with fifteen coins.

17
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:25.845
Blue Phial gets one more for going second, and three for playing on Hard.

18
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.585
This is the calendar, with a pawn for each shop.

19
00:01:30.585 --> 00:01:34.213
Every action moves your pawn forward by its number of days.

20
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:38.780
Whoever is furthest behind always moves next, so after a long action,

21
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.548
your rival may take several turns in a row.

22
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.977
Every even day has a doorbell: when your pawn passes one, a customer walks into your shop.

23
00:01:48.977 --> 00:01:52.983
Carts mark the market days, and day twenty-eight is the Moon Fair.

24
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.209
The market sells four common ingredients, each on a price ladder.

25
00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:03.053
You always take the cheapest token left, so prices climb as stock runs low,

26
00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:05.843
until market day puts four of each back.

27
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.841
The bestiary shows creature sightings. Rare potions need creature parts,

28
00:02:11.841 --> 00:02:16.195
and a hunt costs days, not coins: two to four, one more for a pair,

29
00:02:16.195 --> 00:02:19.176
and one less for the oldest sighting, on the left.

30
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.967
Each recipe in the library exists only once: buy it, and only your shop can brew that potion.

31
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:31.548
Rare recipes cost four coins, Mythic seven, and Legendary ten.

32
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.356
And the Guild buys any potion, at any time, but cheaply.

33
00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:41.535
Every sale knocks a coin off that potion’s price, and market day brings it back up.

34
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.129
On the right is your shop: your actions, then your waiting customers. Each wants healing,

35
00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:51.525
protection, travel or charm, and shows what they pay for each quality:

36
00:02:51.525 --> 00:02:56.661
eleven coins for a common potion, nineteen for a Rare one, plus a small tip.

37
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.972
Customers only wait until the day on their card, then give up and leave.

38
00:03:02.972 --> 00:03:06.849
Our first two, Lady Vane and Old Mortimer, both want healing.

39
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.673
Below are your shelf, which holds four potions, your sales, your pantry, and your recipe book.

40
00:03:13.673 --> 00:03:18.624
Every shop knows the four common recipes, plus one Rare recipe of its own.

41
00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:22.181
Ours is the Unicorn Remedy: only we can brew it.

42
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.656
And at the bottom, the lane ledger records every move, by every shop.

43
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.720
On your move you take one action, and each button shows its cost in days.

44
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.532
A Healing Draught takes two Silverleaf and a Starlight dew, and our pantry is empty.

45
00:03:38.532 --> 00:03:40.233
So, off to market!

46
00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.095
You can buy up to six tokens per trip.

47
00:03:44.095 --> 00:03:48.128
The one-coin spaces start empty, so these cost two coins each.

48
00:03:48.128 --> 00:03:54.117
Two Silverleaf and a dew for the Healing Draught, then two Moonstone and a Glowcap for a Warding Tonic.

49
00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.604
Why a Warding Tonic?

50
00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:02.064
Both of Blue Phial’s customers want protection, so Blue might buy ours.

51
00:04:02.064 --> 00:04:05.131
The trip takes one day. Twelve coins: buy!

52
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.620
Now Blue Phial is furthest behind, on day zero, so it moves.

53
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.879
Its moves pop up at the bottom, and go in the ledger.

54
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.311
Blue Phial went to market, for 16 coins. Then, still behind us on day one,

55
00:04:20.311 --> 00:04:23.000
it brewed a Healing Draught and a Warding Tonic,

56
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.131
and Grum bought the Warding Tonic for 13 coins.

57
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.403
Our move. Brewing makes one or two potions, in two days.

58
00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.871
We pick the Healing Draught.

59
00:04:33.871 --> 00:04:40.180
A waiting customer will buy it as soon as it’s on the shelf, and pays by quality, whatever the tag says.

60
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.903
Then the Warding Tonic. Its price tag is what another shop pays for it.

61
00:04:45.903 --> 00:04:51.807
Blue’s customer would pay Blue thirteen, so the game suggests ten: a profit for both of us.

62
00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.936
Let’s brew!

63
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.385
Sold!

64
00:04:58.385 --> 00:05:04.659
Old Mortimer leaves soonest, so he buys the Healing Draught: eleven coins, plus a two-coin tip.

65
00:05:04.659 --> 00:05:07.171
Lady Vane will wait until day eight.

66
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:14.845
Our pawn also passed the day-two doorbell: Sir Roland the knight wants a Rare healing potion, or better.

67
00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.017
Our Unicorn Remedy is a Rare healing potion.

68
00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:23.791
Tap any recipe, in your book or in the library, to see everything it needs.

69
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.396
It takes a Unicorn hair, a Silverleaf and a Starlight dew, with where each comes from,

70
00:05:29.396 --> 00:05:31.957
its price right now, and whether you have it.

71
00:05:31.957 --> 00:05:36.624
No unicorn has been sighted yet, but new creatures arrive on market day.

72
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.733
Further down: only our shop can brew it, and Sir Roland would pay us twenty-one coins for one.

73
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.148
Both pawns are on day three now.

74
00:05:45.148 --> 00:05:49.857
When pawns share a day, the one that arrived last moves first, and that is us.

75
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.113
Our Warding Tonic sits on the shelf with its price tag.

76
00:05:54.113 --> 00:05:57.073
Changing a tag is free, and costs no days.

77
00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.100
Lady Vane still wants healing, but Blue Phial has a Healing Draught on its shelf.

78
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.596
Shopping at a rival buys up to two potions at their tags, in one day.

79
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.340
Blue tagged it at eight coins.

80
00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.196
Lady Vane will pay us eleven, so that’s a three-coin profit.

81
00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.775
Bought! It lands on our shelf, and Lady Vane snaps it up for eleven coins.

82
00:06:20.775 --> 00:06:18.360
Now it is Blue Phial’s move.

83
00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:22.436
On Blue Phial’s move, the calendar reached day four: market day.

84
00:06:22.436 --> 00:06:28.688
The market restocks, Guild prices recover, and the oldest creature wanders off while a new one is sighted.

85
00:06:28.688 --> 00:06:32.459
A pair of unicorns! The library swaps a recipe, too.

86
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.028
Then Blue Phial went to market. Now look at our banner: just before market day,

87
00:06:42.028 --> 00:06:46.381
Blue Phial bought our Warding Tonic for ten coins, for its own customer.

88
00:06:46.381 --> 00:06:48.296
The coins came straight to us.

89
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.520
Now for those unicorns. Tap a sighting in the bestiary to plan a hunt.

90
00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.929
A pair of unicorns takes four days: three, plus one for the pair.

91
00:06:59.929 --> 00:07:03.700
It brings back two Unicorn hairs, enough for two remedies.

92
00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.895
On the way, our pawn passes two doorbells, but a queue only holds three customers.

93
00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.468
Let’s go hunting.

94
00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.721
Pip the bard came in on day six, but on day eight the queue was full, so that customer walked on by.

95
00:07:18.721 --> 00:07:14.080
Now Blue Phial gets several turns in a row.

96
00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.367
Blue Phial brewed a Healing Draught and a Sleeping Tonic,

97
00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:20.824
and Shady Nell bought the Sleeping Tonic for eleven coins.

98
00:07:20.824 --> 00:07:25.049
Then it bought the recipe for the Invisibility Draught, for seven coins.

99
00:07:25.049 --> 00:07:26.985
Now only Blue can brew it.

100
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.267
Then the calendar reached day eight: another market day.

101
00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.080
Blue Phial went to market, for seven coins.

102
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.636
Our move. We have the Unicorn hairs, but need two Silverleaf and two Starlight dew.

103
00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:46.108
After the restock, all four cost just five coins.

104
00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:53.561
Blue Phial was already on day nine, so we arrived last and move again.

105
00:07:53.561 --> 00:07:55.839
Let’s brew two Unicorn Remedies.

106
00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.969
Sir Roland pays twenty-one coins for his Rare potion.

107
00:08:02.969 --> 00:08:08.923
And Old Mortimer is back, wanting any healing potion, so he takes the other remedy for nineteen.

108
00:08:08.923 --> 00:08:10.965
Forty coins from one brew!

109
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.655
Better potions pay far more, and since only we know this recipe,

110
00:08:16.655 --> 00:08:20.340
any shop with a Rare healing customer has to buy from us.

111
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.735
Meanwhile, with our pawn on day eleven, Blue Phial caught up.

112
00:08:25.735 --> 00:08:32.349
It brewed Night-Eye Drops and a Warding Tonic, and Shady Nell bought the Night-Eye Drops for 13 coins.

113
00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:37.997
Then it spent three days hunting a basilisk, and Grum bought the Warding Tonic for eleven coins.

114
00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:45.183
A few more actions. Buy a recipe costs coins and a day. Phoenix Tears,

115
00:08:45.183 --> 00:08:47.616
a Mythic healing potion, costs seven,

116
00:08:47.616 --> 00:08:51.408
and the dialog shows what it pays and which creatures it needs.

117
00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.383
Mind the shop earns one coin a day,

118
00:08:55.383 --> 00:08:58.712
and moves your pawn to one day past the next pawn ahead:

119
00:08:58.712 --> 00:09:01.245
handy when nothing else is worth doing.

120
00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.416
And Sell to the Guild turns your shelf into coins, in one day.

121
00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.687
Everything is explained in the rulebook, one click away from the Rules button,

122
00:09:11.687 --> 00:09:13.964
in chapters like a real rulebook.

123
00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.732
There are chapters on the calendar, on every action and its cost in days,

124
00:09:19.732 --> 00:09:22.649
and on customers and selling to other shops.

125
00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.583
Ingredients, creatures and recipes lists every beast and every potion,

126
00:09:28.583 --> 00:09:32.055
and the last chapters cover the Moon Fair and a quick reference.

127
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.799
Let’s skip to the end. When every pawn reaches day twenty-eight,

128
00:09:37.799 --> 00:09:42.465
the Guild buys every potion left on a shelf, and the richest shop wins.

129
00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:50.112
The totals stay hidden until the Guild has counted the coins, so nothing gives the result away early.

130
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.132
So close! Blue Phial won by three coins, 92 to 89.

131
00:09:56.132 --> 00:10:01.609
It started with four more, so we actually made more profit, but on Hard every coin counts.

132
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:07.064
The ledger shows where each shop’s money came from, and what it spent.

133
00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:14.563
From here you can look back at the lane, play again, or see your game history.

134
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:20.921
Remember: watch your customers’ patience, price your shelf for the shop next door,

135
00:10:20.921 --> 00:10:23.419
and count the days each action costs.

136
00:10:23.419 --> 00:10:27.943
Your game saves as you play, and you can keep several going from the game lobby.

137
00:10:27.943 --> 00:10:29.964
Good luck, and happy brewing!

